WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:11.620
- Next up, though, is a live interview
with researchers working on conversational agents,

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:13.910
one of the areas

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:16.290
in which Microsoft
Research is a pioneer.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:20.060
I'm having a conversation today
with Lucy Vanderwende.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:21.440
I hopefully said that right.
- You did.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.810
- Bill Dolan of Microsoft and,
of course, Alan Ritter of The Ohio State.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.950
I added the "the" in there for you, Alan.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:30.370
- Thank you.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:32.640
- Assistant professor from
The Ohio State University.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.750
They are going to talk to us today
about, well, talking.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.910
They did a lot of research
in conversational systems.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.760
Welcome, Lucy, Bill, and Alan.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:41.270
How you doing?

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:42.710
- Great.
- Doing well, thank you.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:43.150
- Fantastic.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:47.360
So, what are some of the interesting things
happening in conversational agents?

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.160
Anybody can take that one.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:55.440
- Well, in the past in NLP,
what we used to do is build models

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.930
that really involved a lot of hand

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.860
coding to try to decide
how to respond to a given input.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.760
And in the last several years,
the field has been overtaken

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.050
by newer data-driven techniques

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.300
that allow us to train models on huge volumes

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.000
of naturally occurring data,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.610
specifically from social media,

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:14.250
where we can find-
For the first time

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.810
we can really get access
to enormous volumes

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.480
of text involving people

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.370
having a back-and-forth conversation.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:25.500
- I remember the first conversational agent
I ever wrote was the typical ELIZA, you know?

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.260
This isn't that anymore, right?

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.050
What's the difference between ELIZA and the stuff
that we're working on here?

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.170
Why don't you take that, Alan?

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:35.040
- Yeah, so I think ELIZA
is doing something pretty interesting,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.440
but how it's actually
built is really simple.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.470
It's just a very small set of regular
expressions or handwritten rules,

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:43.550
where as here we're
really trying to learn

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:47.060
how to build conversational agents
just from lots of data.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.890
So, learning them using techniques
from statistical machine translation, for example.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.670
- OK, so statistical machine translation
was something that I got to work on a little bit.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:02.540
I'm not understanding the link
between machine translation and a conversational agent.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:03.650
Why don't you take that one?

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.780
- Think of it as there's the language
of the person who's doing the speaking

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.280
and the language of the person
who's doing the hearing.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:15.980
So, for example, you say,
"I'm going to Las Vegas tonight."

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:19.690
I'm going to say, "Have a nice flight," right?

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.230
It's a kind of a translation.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:27.620
You say one thing, and I'm quite conditioned
to say one of a couple of things,

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.760
or you know, "What are you going to do there?"

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.980
So, it's kind of an input
and an output.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:37.990
The difference between conversation
and machine translation

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.150
is that in machine
translation the content

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:41.860
is all given to you,

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.230
and you just have to faithfully
do the translation.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:50.070
With conversation generation,
you're also kind of deciding what to talk about.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:51.430
That's a bit
of a difference, right?

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:55.220
There's some content
selection going on there.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.500
- When I'm thinking of machine learning,
I'm thinking of there's a target output,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.730
or I'm looking for a specific task
that I'm solving.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.940
When I talk about conversation
in generalities,

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.040
I'm having a hard time
nailing down the task.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.510
And when I can't do that,
the computer has a hard time.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.190
So, Bill, what's the task?

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.580
-No, that's the- I mean, your background
in machine translation shows,

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.650
because that is exactly
one of the challenges

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:17.200
that we faced with this work.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.580
As Lucy was saying, in translation,
the semantics of the input defines

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:22.840
the semantics of the output.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.840
You're trying to affect the form,
but the semantics needs to remain

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.540
constant across that transition.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:32.710
In this conversational space,
you're right, it's a wide-open field, potentially.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.880
You can respond in any way
to what I might say to you.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.840
But we are taking advantage
of the fact that, as Lucy was saying,

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.400
there's a huge amount
of predictability in our responses,

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.360
not so much in what we say
but how we say it.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.930
So, for example, if you ask me
a yes or no question,

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.400
there's a lot that's going
to be predictable

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.910
about the form
of that response.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:55.750
- Sure.
- As Lucy was saying,

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.590
there's a lot of sort of polite conversation,

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.170
chit-chat, back and forth
that is highly predictable.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:06.220
As Lucy was saying, if I say
I'm going to the airport, you're likely to say,

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:08.050
"Have a nice flight,"
or "Where are you going?"

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:10.250
or "Are you going on vacation?"
Something like that.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:11.950
And what we have done...

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:17.420
In translation, there's a metric
that has been very powerful in helping decide

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:19.290
whether a change you've made
to the algorithm

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.980
was good or not, BLEU,

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.750
which is an automated metric
[ indistinct ].

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:30.640
And we've adapted BLEU in this conversational space
to allow ourselves to, in an automated way,

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.030
decide probably
whether that change

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.820
we just made to the algorithm
was good or not.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.290
- And you said it's called BLEU?

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:40.730
- BLEU in translation is the algorithm- I'm sorry,
is the evaluation metric.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:45.150
- So, maybe I'm a little old or I didn't remember BLEU,
but can you talk a little bit

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.090
about what that metric is
and how it actually applies?

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:49.720
Because again, I'm having a hard time.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.120
I know you mentioned it.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.880
I'm having a hard time matching the two.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.510
Why don't you take that?
- Sure, sure, yes.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:56.880
So, BLEU is like an automatic evaluation metric.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.710
One way you could evaluate translations
is to have people look

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.600
at a translation,
like an expert translator,

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.550
and say is this
a good translation, right?

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:08.810
Whereas BLEU will actually compare
the translation against a reference translation

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.600
and try to determine
whether it's correct,

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.840
because there's a lot of different ways
you could translate a sentence.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.400
- By reference, that means
a human-generated translation.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:16.040
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:19.960
- And by the time you take a sentence
in English and translate it 10 times into Japanese --

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.270
10 different people translate
that into Japanese --

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.230
you've begun to cover the space of plausible
translation into Japanese.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:27.230
- Exactly.
- And now, you can look

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.820
for n-gram overlaps,

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:34.670
so those word sequence overlaps between
the way your system translates

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:37.240
that input sentence
and the way humans did.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:40.250
And we've done something very similar
in this conversational space,

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:44.530
looking again on social media
for inputs that have,

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:47.660
across this huge body
of text of data,

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:50.740
many different responses
produced by many different people,

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:52.060
so we can do something
very similar.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.340
Given this input, we predict
the response is this.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:59.540
Now, compare sequences of words
in that predicted response

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.530
to what we found
across the corpus

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.600
for what people
actually said

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.690
when "I'm going to the airport"
was uttered as the input.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:10.750
And so, again, the space
of possible responses is infinite,

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:14.270
but you can begin to cover it
in this way if you have enough data.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:17.030
- Yeah, I think in conversation,
the space of outputs

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:18.690
is much larger
than translation, right?

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:22.080
So, in translation, the input and output
are semantically equivalent,

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.050
so it's kind of a more
constrained problem than conversation.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:26.060
- But it's not
totally infinite, right?

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:35.000
Because conversation, successful conversation,
runs along their cooperative principles,

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.620
and I'm not going
to be redundant.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.150
So, if you ask me a question...

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:43.700
Like, ELIZA pretty much worked
because ELIZA asked the question

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.440
you are compelled
to answer, right?

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:50.730
So, you are getting tricked into- not tricked,
but- - Falling into the rut.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.230
- But you fall into the rut.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:57.410
That's what we as cooperative people
in a conversation do.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:02.640
And so, to be cooperative also when you ask me a-
If you say something, I'm not going to say,

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.510
"Oh, yeah, my hair pin is blue,"

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.350
because it's a total non sequitur.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:11.220
So, the space of things that are relevant
to say in a conversation is not infinite.

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.670
It's not deterministic.

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:15.230
I can say a couple of things.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:19.390
I have some flexibility, but I'm not going to totally
go off the rails, either.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.620
And that's what we do as people.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:28.810
When you say something, you kind of predict
what I might say, what I might start talking about.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:35.700
But the thing that makes it interesting is
when we slightly veer off-topic,

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:38.270
when I introduce a new theme,

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:39.950
and that makes conversation engaging.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:45.560
- Again, my challenge that I'm having
is when I'm doing translation,

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.910
there is a specific output.

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.830
And you mentioned nine
reference translations, right?

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:53.390
But when we're looking
at conversational topics...

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.550
Like, I've had my four-year-old
come out of left field and say something.

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:59.540
- Yeah, I mean, let me just interrupt,
because I completely agree

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:00.440
with everything you're saying,

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:02.680
and I also did not know this
approach was going to work.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.820
But Michel Galley, who's in our research group,
is a machine translation person.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:12.920
He was completely convinced that this same kind
of automated metric would work in this space.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:17.340
And to my surprise, it does,
because so much of the response is predictable,

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.620
even if the specific
content is not.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.790
If you say, "Are you going?" the response
is very likely to be "Yes, I am,"

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:26.850
"No, I'm not," something or other.

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:30.260
And just that "Yes, I am," "No, I'm not" is enough
to give us some signal as to

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.550
whether we're probably
doing something reasonable

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.290
with this algorithm or not.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:37.700
And it's really critical to have
an automated metric like this,

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.940
because it's the combination --
I would say;

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:42.680
maybe you want to correct me
if you disagree, but...

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:45.440
I think translation really began
to leap forward in quality...

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:46.150
- Oh, no question.

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:48.320
- ...when you got the confluence

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.700
of a lot of data,
parallel data,

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:55.480
that automated metric,
and better machines.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:09:01.110
I think that troika allowed the field
to leap forward, because without that automated metric,

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:03.380
BLEU, in the old days,

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:06.650
you had to wait for people
to manually score your translations.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:09.820
It would take a lot of money
and a lot of time to decide

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:11.950
whether that tweak you made
yesterday was a good thing.

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:17.110
But once you could hit a button and get an instant read
on whether you made probably an improvement or not,

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:18.870
things really began to accelerate.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.710
And in this conversational space,
again, that evaluation,

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:23.690
finding an automated
evaluation metric,

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:26.340
is a critical component
to really making progress

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:27.650
in this machine learning area.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:31.130
- So, when I started machine translation,
we did this thing called a noisy channel model.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:32.610
Is it the same thing?

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.300
Because I don't know what state of the art
is in machine translation anymore.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.290
- Yeah, so when we first started doing this,
when I was first doing an internship at

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:41.450
MSR- - So, you were actually
at Microsoft as an intern?

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:42.530
- Yes.
- That's awesome.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:44.470
- Yeah, it's awesome.
- Tell us about that.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:48.630
- This was, like, 2010,
and this was really the first attempt in the field

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:50.590
to build a data-driven dialog-

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.750
- That's exactly
when I finished grad school.

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:58.320
No, actually, I was in till 2012,
so that was right around the time that I was...

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:03.120
- Yeah, what Alan did was really the first attempt
to take parallel data in the form of input-response

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.270
and learn a translation
model from it

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:07.010
that's going to predict
[ indistinct ].

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:07.880
- So, tell us more about that.

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:10.270
- Yeah, so I mean, I think I was just really
in the right place at the right time.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:13.490
I think Microsoft Research was one of the first places
really investing a lot of effort

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.630
in this data-driven conversation thing.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:20.020
And yeah, when we first started out,
we were using these noisy channel type models.

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:26.340
But recently, the neural network translation models
have become more popular for both machine translation

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.470
and in conversations.

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:34.010
- Is that because the deep learning models
are better at taking a lot of input, or...?

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:37.710
I'm trying to understand, because like I said,
right at 2010, you remember,

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:40.880
that's when everyone was like,
"Oh, neural networks don't work."

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:42.300
And then, all of a sudden,
they really did,

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:43.850
because of the depth and...

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:47.010
- It's actually interesting.
I think the models that Alan built

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:49.500
tended to produce more
interesting responses,

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.300
but they weren't very fluent.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:55.580
And with the advent of neural models,
that same data dumped into the neural models produces-

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:56.580
- So, this was smoothing.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:00.140
- Yeah, exactly, very fluent
responses that tend to be bland.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:06.900
The model tends to produce this
very dull, statistically safe response.

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:10.480
The big challenge has been to try to goad it
into saying something more interesting.

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:12.880
- Do like a Boltzmann sum, push it out of its state.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:20.500
- But it might also be that it's helping us
to differentiate how we talk from what we say, right?

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:21.830
So, there's two axes.

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:25.180
It's how we say things -- is it English, is it French?

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:26.590
-- and what is it that we're saying.

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:29.020
We're saying the same thing
in these various languages.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:34.120
And so, the neural models are really good
at learning how we say things,

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:39.630
and we're now working towards
improving our ability

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:41.460
to control what it says.

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:47.870
- So, I mean, it feels like you're trying
to inject some kind of personality into it somehow.

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:48.210
Is that...?

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:50.990
- Right, there's personality,
there's grounding...

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:58.820
So, Bill's previous example was always,
"Is the Starbucks open?"

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:01.460
Well, that depends
on where you are.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:02.540
Are we in Seattle?

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:04.290
What time is it?
- Oh, I see.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:07.520
- The answer needs to be grounded, right?

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:09.270
So, that's the what it's going to say.

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.370
How it's going to say that -- "Yes, it is,"

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:15.440
or "Hey, there's only 10 minutes
left when it's open" --

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.100
that's the how we're saying things.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:20.860
- And it's worth saying that I think this grounding
is critically important, as Lucy was saying,

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:25.140
that it's really vital that we connect
these conversations to the world,

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:26.770
or it's just blather.

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:32.730
I think traditionally in natural language processing,
we tended to map from language into language

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.020
whether it was translation
or summarization

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:37.930
or spelling, grammar correction,
and that kind of thing.

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:42.320
- But I think, even this, there are some sort
of useful applications, like if I get a text message.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:46.190
If my friend says, "I'm feeling sick," you can maybe
predict how to respond to them

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:49.050
without even seeing any input
that they're typing in,

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:52.850
sort of then click confirm
and send to respond.

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:56.100
- I feel like we've talked about this a lot,
and I want to see something now,

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:57.840
and I want you to talk to it, Bill.

00:12:57.840 --> 00:12:59.470
Looks like you've got some
on your computer here.

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:00.290
Let's see.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:01.610
He's put his glasses down.
It's going to be good.

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:03.140
- Yeah, let me just try an example.

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:05.690
Well, we can do the one that Lucy...

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:07.550
Sorry.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:12.850
Something kind of frequent,
like "I'm feeling really sick."

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:15.160
- I'm feeling really sick today.

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:15.660
OK.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.140
- Which is really common, you know.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:19.040
You're going to see that...

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:20.930
Sorry, this is a little slow
over the network, I think.

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:22.080
Here we go.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:26.810
Again, this is not being retrieved
from a corpus of responses.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.940
This is being made up by
the neural model, word by word.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:33.420
This is a very high-frequency kind of exchange.

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:33.800
Oops.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:39.430
- And that's cool, because usually
when I'm doing stuff with machine learning,

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:40.560
there's a specific task.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.360
But you just literally typed in
"I'm feeling sick today,"

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:50.050
and to me, that's not input that
I would give a computer, right?

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:52.330
But it's cool that you've been able
to actually model

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:55.040
the typical responses
for that kind of thing.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:57.120
Can you show us some more examples?

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:59.050
- Yeah, so why don't I just
show you something that...

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:04.190
Let's see, something like,
"My mother is coming to visit."

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:07.160
Actually, I'm going to do-
I'm going to restart the context,

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:09.200
so it's not affected by the prior term.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:14.460
- So, when you say it's not affected,
does it keep like a stack of information?

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:19.440
- Yeah, in various experiments, we're taking advantage
of prior context to influence what's said.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:26.030
I really just want to point out the pronoun here,
that it's getting "she."

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:27.370
First of all, it's a well formed sentence,

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:28.700
and the pronoun is right.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:33.030
If I said something like, "My father is..."
Well, let's try that over here.

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:36.120
My father is coming to visit.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:45.520
- So, there's a deep model...
- He.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:46.980
- I'm sorry?
- So, you see "he,"

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:52.410
which again is very low-level
kind of experience to show.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:56.000
But it is really fascinating to me
that it's getting the words right,

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.610
it's doing the lexical choice very nicely,

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:00.180
it's getting the word order right,
it's getting the pronoun right,

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:02.360
and it's very good at this kind of pattern.

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:05.250
- I mean, indirect object pronouns,
when I was doing research,

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:06.960
was the bane of NLP's existence, right?

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:10.800
- Right, and you would have imagined that
you'd have to write a rule, right?

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:11.150
- Yeah.

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:15.720
- You would imagine that we needed
to do coreference resolution and all of that,

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:17.780
but it's just falling out
from the model.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:22.030
Because the models, because they've been
trained on such huge amounts of data,

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:24.270
they've learned how to talk.

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:25.300
- I see.

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:28.550
So, what can this be used for?

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:30.630
Because right now, to me...

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:35.410
If someone's watching this that
doesn't know about NLP and hasn't done research in this

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:38.000
and is not completely blown away --

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:41.850
because I am -- what would this be used for?

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:44.770
- Yeah, do you want to...?
- I think, like I sort of mentioned before,

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:47.270
like if you want to
automatically generate replies

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:49.760
to an email or a text message
you could sort of just- you know,

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:53.390
it could generate something and just click send
if it's a common response.

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:58.780
I think there's also a lot of opportunity to integrate
this into more goal-directed dialog systems,

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:00.980
so it's more like natural.

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:04.470
Instead of feeling like really talking to your phone,
it's more like you're talking to a person

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:06.320
and having a natural conversation.

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:06.880
- I see.
Bill?

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:07.840
- No, I was going to say

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:09.890
that I think this notion of grounding
is really important,

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:12.320
and that's where the leap to neural models

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:14.980
from the older statistical models
is really important.

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:17.870
Because in these neural models
where everything's a vector,

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:20.560
we can entangle
linguistic information

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:22.080
with real-world information.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.590
Features from a photo,
something else in your environment,

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:26.580
the state of your machine,
all of that kind of thing

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:28.770
can be entangled
in this model

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:31.160
that now knows something
about both language

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:34.320
and its intersection
with your program state.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:37.380
So, we can begin to now move away
from this kind of chit-chat scenario.

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:40.010
We're really not trying
to build a chit-chat model here.

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:45.100
As Alan was saying, we're trying to build
a model that is aware of your world around you,

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:46.870
but also knows
how to engage you

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:49.400
in a turn-taking conversation.

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:55.090
And its responses can be influenced, and potentially
your responses can influence the state of the world.

00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:56.340
- Is there a way- I'm sorry, go ahead.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:57.800
- I was just going to say, again,

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:02.480
if you follow the cooperative
principle of conversations,

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:05.750
you don't want a computer
that robotically says,

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:08.210
"I am talking
at this moment at..."

00:17:08.210 --> 00:17:12.370
We leave out a lot
of information as well,

00:17:12.370 --> 00:17:15.150
because maybe you've already
told me that information.

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:21.500
So, we also want it to-
the how needs to be natural.

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:26.770
The more natural we can get
the conversational agent to be,

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:29.470
the more you're willing
to interact with it

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:33.690
and to share with
a conversational agent information

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:37.630
about you that will help it
do its task later on.

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:40.750
- I think we're coming at this
from this perspective that we're not trying

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:42.480
to build either
a chit-chat engine

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.700
or a purely transactional,

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:46.270
task-oriented dialog engine.

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:48.280
We're trying to blend both
into the same model.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:51.940
- So, you're saying you can take this
conversational model and blend it with,

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:54.550
for example, your insurance
company bot.

00:17:54.550 --> 00:17:57.260
And what it will do is you can start
talking insurance to it,

00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:00.520
and so you get the model
for insurance knowledge

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:02.380
with this sort of smoothing

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:03.880
model that's smoothing
the real conversation.

00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:06.620
- Yeah, if you think about renting
a car at an airport,

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:08.600
most of that conversation
may be transactional,

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:12.190
but there's likely to be some
sort of polite sugar in there.

00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:14.850
The person behind the counter will say,
"Oh, you're going to the Grand Canyon?

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:17.100
The weather's beautiful out there today."
Something like that.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:21.260
It's relevant to the conversation,
but it's not relevant to deciding what car

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:23.250
you're going to get or how many days
you're going to have it.

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:25.130
So, we're aiming more
for that combined model.

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:27.000
- Well, this has been really awesome.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:28.500
Apparently, we ran out of time.

00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:30.930
This is good stuff.

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:34.150
I mean, it touches to the heart of some of the stuff
that I got to work on.

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:35.490
This is really impressive work.

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:37.530
Thanks so much for spending
some time with us.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:40.110
Thank you, Bill and Lucy, for your time
and fascinating discussion.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:43.730
We look forward to hearing more about
conversational agents in the future.

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:45.890
Also, not forgetting the professor.

